.TH HSEARCH_R 3
.SH NAME
hcreate_r, hsearch_r, hdestroy_r \- reentrant hash-table search
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <search.h>
.PP
.BI "int hcreate_r(size_t " nel ", struct hsearch_data *" htab ");"
.br
.BI "int hsearch_r(ENTRY " item ", ACTION " action ", ENTRY **" retval ,
.BI "struct hsearch_data *" htab ");"
.br
.BI "void hdestroy_r(struct hsearch_data *" htab ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the reentrant equivalents of
.BR hcreate (3),
.BR hsearch (3)
and
.BR hdestroy (3):
each operates on a caller-supplied
.IR htab ,
so a program may hold several hash tables at once and use them from different
threads.
.PP
.B hcreate_r
allocates a table sized for at least
.I nel
entries.
.B hdestroy_r
frees it.
.PP
.B hsearch_r
searches
.I htab
for an entry matching the key
.IR item.key .
If
.I action
is
.BR FIND ,
a matching entry is reported through
.I *retval
or the call fails if none exists.  If
.I action
is
.BR ENTER ,
.I item
is inserted when absent (and the existing entry is reported when present).
An
.B ENTRY
is a
.RI { "char *key" ", " "void *data" }
pair.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Following the glibc convention \(em the inverse of most
.I _r
functions \(em
.B hcreate_r
and
.B hsearch_r
return nonzero on success and 0 on error, with
.I errno
set.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOMEM
.B hcreate_r
could not allocate the table, or
.B hsearch_r
with
.B ENTER
found the table full.
.TP
.B ESRCH
.B hsearch_r
with
.B FIND
found no matching entry.
.TP
.B EINVAL
A NULL argument, or
.B hcreate_r
called on a table already in use.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bsearch (3),
.BR lsearch (3),
.BR tsearch (3)
.SH STANDARDS
GNU extension (the reentrant forms of the POSIX
.BR hsearch (3)
family).
